okay so on monday a cs go team named  chaos esports club defeated mibr  in hard-fought best-of-three series as  part of cs summit's online qualifier  but almost immediately accusations of  cheating were leveled against chaos  by mostly brazilian fans particularly  aimed  at leaf their youngest player now leaf  started receiving  waves of abuse and even death threats on  social media  he is 16 years old and this has prompted  a crucial question that we  as part of the cs go community need to  try  to answer how do we address the question  of whether or not someone's cheating  without enabling abuse harassment  and death threats by angry unhinged fans  okay guys so as you may or may not know  we are streaming on twitch pretty  regularly these days  so go ahead and head over to twitch.tv  slash the score esports give us a follow  and turn notifications on  and obviously if you haven't already  subscribe to this youtube channel now  the first thing i want to say about this  leaf situation is that i am not here  today  to address the validity of the cheating  accusations that have been leveled  against them  we are here today to talk about the ugly  aftermath  in fact we're not even going to show you  the clips that function as the alleged  proof that this young man  cheated if you want to see them they're  out there and easy enough to find on  your own  because right now the issue is not  whether he did  or did not cheat or has ever cheated  right at this very moment the issue is  how we  as a community address these situations  going forward and how we got to the  point where a 16 year old kid  is being sent death threats from beating  a brazilian team  in a qualifier let's back up for a quick  second to where this all started  after the match on monday leaf tweets  out 2-1 against mibr ggs to them  we all had insane maps plus a little  joke about steel  the replies to that tweet come in hot  and heavy  a lot of them being angry accusations of  cheating from  mostly brazilian fans for those of you  who aren't quite as steeped in your cs  go history  brazilian fans and crowds have a  reputation for being  pretty wild and at times pretty  controversial  land events in brazil feature absolutely  rabid fans screaming for their team and  booing the enemy  and sometimes doing a little more than  that sometimes calling out crucial  information  or making map callouts that would give  their favorite team an unfair advantage  and to be fair they are definitely not  the only nationality  that has been known to do this on home  turf there is a passion to brazilian  fans  that is often celebrated in the cs go  community as it  should be but sometimes that passion  can cross lines in a big way they found  my  my mom and my sister's facebook and they  were literally spamming them  like that same week before i was in the  go of  if you come to brazil we're going to  kill you you know we hate you  brazil hates you and that's exactly what  happened to leaf of chaos esports club  the angry aggressive toxic tweets  started spilling over into outright  death threats  death threats that thankfully have since  been deleted by twitter as they should  be  but if you go to that original leaf  tweet you can see the  hundreds of toxic accusatory  harassing messages and that's only what  we see publicly i think it's pretty  obvious how all of us should feel when  it comes to a  16 year old getting death threats for  beating a brazilian team and an online  qualifier no matter what the  circumstances  but i talked to sadikist about this  entire issue recently and  i think he put it best but i was more  surprised to see the reaction of the  community being so hostile  um and maybe i shouldn't be um  the problem being is that we had players  in this case that have  said not to call people out before  because it does this doing the exact  same thing obviously in the case of  fallen that that's that's pretty true  and i think that  they're a little bit uh  it's hard to say that they still are in  2020 but i think a little bit ignorant  of what they're following is capable of  what  what implications that has this is  unfortunately  nowhere near the first time a cs go  professional  has received death threats from the fan  community  but i think what was really  disappointing is seeing other pros and  influencers add fuel to this fire by  weighing in on whether or not  leaf was cheating in this match the same  day the match took place  tarik who was himself part of the mibr  squad  back at the end of 2018 tweeted several  clips from that match and heavily  implied  that he felt that leaf was cheating  fallen himself chimed in with a pretty  damning tweet as well  and they weren't the only ones either  gaulis a 1.6  pro and a streamer with a massive  brazilian counter-strike audience  piled on his own speculations as well i  mean some of the clips where he's  evaluating whether or not  the players from chaos esports club  cheated have tens of thousands of views  and by the time you see this footage  probably more now are any of these guys  telling  anyone to go out and threaten members of  chaos esports club  no of course they're not but by weighing  in  by implying or outright stating that  leaf or anyone else was cheating  they create a climate where it's okay to  let  accusations fly guys like fallen and  guys like tarik  who are pros and have a massive  following need to be aware of their  influence and it's hard to imagine that  in 2020 that they  aren't and fallen being on a team like  mibr should know that and if you don't  in 2020 then you need to have pr  addressed within the team and the  organizations maybe need to look at it  because  it's hard to imagine someone that's been  around that long  saying oh well you know i can make that  tweet and it's not a big deal no he has  to know that people are going to react  and they're going to take his side due  to his stance in the scene and he has to  know what he's implicating now it's  worth stating that all three guys i  mentioned walked back their original  statements on social media  and asked people not to go out and  harass anyone because of them  but honestly at this point it's too  little too late the damage has been done  pro players and influencers have to  understand that what they say and do  has a huge influence on what fans say  and do  even if they aren't explicitly telling  anyone to actually  do anything by throwing out accusations  like this  they can make certain people feel okay  about taking it too far  this is something the ceo of chaos  esports club greg  laird talked about in a recent tweet  where he said  fallen taric immortals mibr all of your  behavior is unacceptable immature  and does nothing except gathering the  pitchforks of a community  report it to an admin if you think one  of our players is cheating  unbelievable players on other titles are  receiving threatening dms  because of your actions you have a huge  platform use it  responsibly now i did speak to laird the  ceo of krc sports for this story and  although his team wasn't quite ready to  sit down for an interview just yet  he did give me a statement on the matter  which i'll share with you now  the backlash and attitude of the  community against nathan has been  incredibly disappointing  but happily quite a few members of the  community have reached out to offer the  team  and nathan support all i'd like to add  is that pro players and community  members  have large platforms no matter what is  said after the fact  it is very easy for these accusations or  speculations to spin out of control  into very personally damaging situations  for the players and staff  our staff myself and even my girlfriend  have all received  private messages about this that were  less than polite  the team is hard to upset and has  handled the situation very well  all things considered and we've just got  so many examples from cs go's past to  draw on  of players who have been accused of  cheating and who have suffered  personally as a result i mean you guys  could probably name a bunch off the top  of your head  flusha comes to mind rops rops who let's  face it was harassed and bullied  until he literally went to face it  headquarters  and played fpl from there to prove he  wasn't cheating  the people who i looked up to started  calling me a cheater then  it wasn't good i i didn't really want to  play at that point  because it's not good when you look up  to a person and then  suddenly he starts calling you a cheater  just put yourself in  any of these people's shoes you're a  young man you're trying to do what you  love  and all of a sudden you can't turn left  right or sideways  without seeing a horrible aggressive  harassing insensitive message from a  member of the community  because they think you're cheating look  i don't know if leaf is cheating  and neither do you and neither do tarik  fallen  or gallus what we do know is that there  are people out there who will cross  lines who will threaten abuse and harass  pro players when their heroes seem to  give them  implicit permission to do so okay sure  you may have lost that game and  and maybe you're looking for a way to  deflect some of the hatred coming toward  you  um so you you put out this accusation  you gotta you gotta realize we have  proper channels for that stuff we have  admins and they should be the ones to at  least make the decision if someone's  gonna put out an accusation  they should be the ones to do it and the  pro players should know to go through  those channels and keep in mind  a 16 year old kid i mean fallen should  know this more than anyone is trying to  make a living and trying to pursue his  dream within counter-strike  and now he's on the back foot for the  rest of his potential career uh  he's got people that even if he  apologizes even if he's cleared of this  there will be people that just because  they will troll i mean we have people  online that have  large followings that literally that's  all they do and they hide behind a  persona and they harass people and this  will make him  more defensive moving forward less open  to to to being  friendly with the community and maybe  even him you know take the  sweetness out of his mouth for  counter-strike and he might get bitter  about it and not want to pursue i mean  this is it has  huge repercussions for someone who's  essentially still in their formative  years of counter-strike  so usually at the end of the video this  is what we call the end board  we do a gag we show a blooper i talk  about something on my shelf  we give you a little lighthearted  insight into sort of like who we are as  people  but for this one i wanted to actually  say something that's a little bit more  serious  i mean my i myself and some of my  colleagues  have been at times in our careers the  target of online harassment  it just comes with the territory when  you're working in a public way like we  do  when you're on camera when you have a  byline on an article doesn't matter if  you do anything wrong  it's going to happen at some point and  let me tell you it sucks man  it sucks when you have to log on to  twitter or instagram or whatever other  social media  and you see messages or tweets from  people who are saying terrible things  about you  even if you know they're not true and  even if you know these people are just  being idiots  it does hurt it feels bad and i can't  imagine how bad it feels if you're  16 years old so keep that in mind guys  and conduct yourselves  in a courteous professional manner when  you're on social media  behind every avatar no matter if it's a  pro or  an egg with one follower these are  real people with real emotions
 

